.docname {Backend runtime}
.include {docs}

How the backend works as a program: what runs at the same time, who may touch what, and what
happens when something goes wrong. [How it runs](how-it-runs.qd) shows the flow; this page gives
the reasons. Words like **map**, **path**, **model** and **twin** are defined in the
[Glossary](glossary.qd).

## The parts

| Part | File | One job |
|---|---|---|
| **Map store** | `io/store` | Keeps the backend's copy of the map and the path |
| **Model worker** | `io/worker` | Runs the model and the output writers on a copy of the map |
| **Web server** | `io/server` | Talks to the robot and the browsers; owns the worker |
| **Offline loader** | `io/offline` | Reads a saved map and path from files |
| **Settings reader** | `io/config` | Reads `model.json` and checks every setting sent later |

`main.cpp` only joins them.

.mermaid caption:{Who owns whom.}
    flowchart TD
        MAIN[main.cpp] --> ST[Map store]
        MAIN --> SRV[Web server]
        SRV --> WK[Model worker]
        SRV -- "writes frames" --> ST
        WK -- "take a copy" --> ST

## Start and stop

1. `main` reads the options: `--port` (default 9002), `--config <file>`, `--map <file.pcd>`.
2. It reads the config file, if any. A wrong file stops the program with a message.
3. With `--map`, it loads the map into the store, once.
4. It reads the **ingest token** from the environment variable `VCORTEX_INGEST_TOKEN`. The token
   is a shared password: a robot must send the same value before the backend takes its map. Empty
   means no check. See [Usage](usage.qd) for how to set it.
5. The server starts the worker thread, then listens.
6. `SIGINT` or `SIGTERM` stops the server cleanly; then the worker is stopped. So
   `docker stop` needs nothing special.

The worker is stopped **after** the server, and it is the **last member** of the server. The
worker's thread calls back into the server; this order makes sure it never reaches a connection
that is already gone.

## The map store

**In short:** the map store holds the backend's copy of the map and the path. Each message from
the robot updates it. Every few seconds the model worker takes a copy and works on that copy, so
the robot never waits for the model.

**Cube and dot.** Space is cut into small **cubes** (5 cm on the robot). A cube is a box of space;
a **dot** is the one point kept for it, at the average position of everything the laser saw inside.
So a cube holds exactly one dot: "a new cube" and "a new dot" mean the same thing.

### How it works inside

```text
cube key = floor(position / cube size)        for x, y and z

example, cube size 0.05 m, a dot at (1.23, -0.02, 0.51):
  key = ( floor(24.6), floor(-0.4), floor(10.2) ) = ( 24, -1, 10 )
```

- A **hash table** maps each key to a place in a **plain list** of dots.
- A new cube goes at the end of the list. A cube seen again **replaces** its dot where it is.
  So a dot's position in the list never changes, and the browser can address the dot by that
  position.
- The dots sit in a list, not inside the table, because the worker copies them all: one block
  copies faster than many small ones.
- A `dirty` flag says "the map changed since the last copy". `take()` gives a copy **and clears
  the flag**; `peek()` gives a copy and leaves it; `touch()` sets it (new settings).
- **One lock** covers everything. A copy is made while holding it, so the model then runs with
  no lock, and an arriving delta never waits for the model.

| Call | Does |
|---|---|
| `reset` | Forgets the map and the path; a new source starts |
| `put_map` | Adds a whole map; a new `run_id` drops everything first |
| `put_delta` | Adds a delta if it follows the last one; says where each dot went |
| `put_poses` | Adds poses, one every 0.2 m at most |
| `take` | Copy if changed, nothing otherwise |
| `peek` | Copy now, for a viewer that just joined |
| `touch` | Marks the map as changed |
| `status` | Run, `seq`, number of dots and poses |

## The model worker

One thread. It holds the model and every **output writer**, one after the other. An output writer
(a **connector** in the code) turns the building into one file format: twin JSON, DXF or PLY (see
[Output formats](output-formats.qd)).

.mermaid caption:{The worker's loop.}
    flowchart TD
        A[Start] --> B[Copy the map<br/>only if it changed]
        B --> C{Anything new?}
        C -- no --> E
        C -- yes --> D[Run the model, then<br/>every output writer]
        D --> D2{Cleared while it ran?}
        D2 -- yes --> E
        D2 -- no --> D3[Keep it as the latest output<br/>and tell the server]
        D3 --> E[Sleep for the period<br/>or until new settings]
        E --> B

- It sleeps **one period** (3 s, from `model_period_s`) after each run, then looks again. It does
  **not** run once per delta. A **delta** is a small message from the robot with only the cubes that
  are new or changed since the last one. A burst of 100 deltas within 3 seconds gets computed in a
  single run, not 100 runs.
- New settings wake it **at once**, so a sleeping worker does not wait out the 3 seconds.
- A run is never stopped halfway. Settings that arrive **during** a run only raise a flag. When the
  run ends, the worker sees the flag and starts the next run right away, without sleeping.
  **Example:** three `PUT`s arrive while a run is going. They give **one** extra run, with the
  values of the last one, not three runs.
- It checks once **before** the first sleep. So in offline mode the twin is ready at start, not
  3 seconds later.
- The output is one object with the bytes of **every format**, shared by pointer. A download and a
  broadcast read the same bytes; nothing is copied.
- The **listener** (the server's `publish`) runs on the worker's thread. A slow listener delays
  the next run.

### What can fail, and what then

| Failure | Result |
|---|---|
| One output writer throws | That format is missing from this run; the others are kept. It is logged on `stderr` |
| The model throws | This run gives no output; the next change runs again. The thread does **not** end, because an exception leaving the thread would end the whole server |
| A new source joins during a run | `clear()` raises a counter. The running job sees the counter changed and **drops its output**: it describes the old map |

## The web server

### Who is connected

| What | Kept as |
|---|---|
| The source | One connection, or none; plus its name |
| Raw viewers | A map from connection to its byte budget and state (see [Protocol](protocol.qd)) |
| Twin viewers | For each format id, the set of its connections |

A **viewer** is one open browser view: a **raw viewer** shows the dots (`/ws/raw`), a **twin
viewer** shows the twin in one format (`/ws/twin`). The **byte budget** is how much the backend
may send one raw viewer each second. All three are in the [Glossary](glossary.qd).

### Where the locks are

| Lock | Guards | Why it exists |
|---|---|---|
| Store lock | The map, the path, the flag | One writer (the source) and one reader (the worker) |
| Worker lock | Settings, latest output, the counter | The server and the worker thread both read them |
| Client lock | The viewer sets and the source | Also held while a frame is applied **and sent**, so a viewer that joins sees each delta either inside its whole map or after it, never neither |
| Settings lock | One settings update | Two `PUT`s read the settings, then write them: without it one update is lost |

### What happens when …

| Event | Result |
|---|---|
| A robot goes quiet and never closes | Its connection stays; the **next valid hello replaces it** |
| A viewer is slow | It skips deltas, then gets a whole map (see [Protocol](protocol.qd)) |
| A viewer joins late | It gets the whole map, or the latest twin, at once |
| A source sends a bad frame | That connection is closed with a reason; the store is left as it was |
| A new run's output arrives after a new source | `publish` drops it if it is no longer the latest |
| A twin is sent to a viewer of a format that failed | Nothing is sent to that viewer for that run |

## Offline mode

`--map <file.pcd>` loads a map once and **refuses every source**. Nothing else changes: the same
store, the same worker, the same viewers.

| Detail | Value |
|---|---|
| Map file | `.pcd`, read by field **name** (`x`, `y`, `z`, `intensity`, `num_scan_seen`); any type of number; other fields are skipped |
| Dots with `NaN` | Dropped (a gap in an organised cloud) |
| Frame name | Always `camera_init`, because a `.pcd` file holds no frame name |
| Cube size | 0.1 mm. A saved cloud need not have one dot per 5 cm cube, so this keeps every dot apart |
| Path | An optional file `trajectory.tum` **next to** the map: one pose per line, `time x y z qx qy qz qw`. Only the yaw is kept |
| No path | The map loads, but the model finds **no room and so no walls**: the free floor is counted from the poses |
| Wrong line in the path | The program stops, naming the file and the line |

**Example, real.** Opening a real map with 192,149 dots and 260 poses: the model ran once, in
1.57 s, and `/api/status` showed `"mode":"offline"`, `"points":192149`, `"poses":260`.
The model runs again only when a setting changes, because the map never does.

## Gaps found

| What | Where |
|---|---|
| `compose.yaml` has a TODO saying `--map` does not exist yet. It does exist. Offline mode in Docker needs the `command` line to be edited by hand | `compose.yaml` |
| A comment in `io/offline.cpp` says that without a path the model uses an "outermost-line rule". In fact `find_floor_rooms` returns nothing when the path is empty | `io/offline.cpp`, `model/floor/free_space.cpp` |
